<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashurnasirpal II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 883–859 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashurnasirpal II was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from 883 to 859 BCE and one of the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>. In yearly campaigns he subdued the Aramaean states along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> and the highlands of Nairi and Zamua, and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, collecting tribute from Patina and the Phoenician cities. His inscriptions boast of brutal punishment of rebels. He rebuilt Kalhu, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, as his capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii.jpg" alt="Ashurnasirpal II" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashurnasipal_with_official.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
